  "properties": {
    "long_instructions": "# Bias in Data \r\n\r\n#### Bias happens when some people, ideas, or situations are represented more than others in a way that is unfair.\r\n\r\n#### In data and AI, bias often comes from who is missing or who is overrepresented in a dataset. If one group appears much more often than others, the results can reflect an uneven or incomplete view of the world.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n<img src=\"https://images.code.org/aa466cc4ed48dcdf28bb18b34243afec-image-1766178239978.png\" alt=\"choice level with animal adoption, community fair, and metro expansion\" width=\"750px\" style=\"display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;border-radius:10px; \">\r\n\r\n\r\n## Connecting to Learning\r\n\r\n#### In lesson 5, students will explore how data can shape fairness in real-world systems by choosing between animal adoption, community events, or metro expansion scenarios. \r\n\r\n#### In each case, the way data was collected and used affected which groups or outcomes were prioritized.\r\n\r\n#### Similarly, when we use `.map()` to create a new dataset, we make decisions about what information to keep and what to leave out.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n> #### **Reflect on one of the following:**\r\n> - #### If a dataset mostly includes some categories and leaves others out, how could that lead to bias when we use that data?\r\n>- #### How can examples like this help students understand that bias can come from data choices, not just from the technology itself?\r\n> - #### Where might similar data choices affect fairness in the real world (for example, in transportation, school programs, or community resources)?\r\n\r\n",
